---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const title = 'Spousal Support FAQ | spousalsupportcalc.com';
const description = 'Common questions about spousal support: who pays, how long, taxes, modification, and termination.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>Spousal Support FAQ</h1>

  <h2>Who pays spousal support?</h2>
  <p>The higher-earning spouse, typically. Gender doesn't matter under modern statutes &mdash; either spouse can pay or receive based on relative incomes and circumstances. In a marriage where one spouse earned $8,000/month and the other $2,000/month, the higher earner is the presumptive payer regardless of which spouse filed for divorce or which spouse is considered "at fault" in states that still weigh conduct.</p>

  <h2>How long does spousal support last?</h2>
  <p>Varies widely. Short marriages (under 5 years) often get little or no permanent support. Marriages over 20 years can lead to indefinite support, especially with significant income gaps. State guidelines often tie duration to a percentage of marriage length &mdash; see our <a href="/methodology/">methodology page</a> for the specific duration bands most states use.</p>

  <h2>Is spousal support taxable?</h2>
  <p>For divorces finalized AFTER 12/31/2018: spousal support is NOT deductible to the payer and NOT taxable income to the recipient (federal). For divorces finalized BEFORE that date: opposite treatment under the old rules, unless modified post-2018.</p>

  <h2>Can spousal support be modified?</h2>
  <p>Yes, with a showing of material change in circumstances. Job loss, retirement, significant income changes, or remarriage typically trigger modification eligibility. Some agreements specify whether support is modifiable.</p>

  <h2>Does remarriage end spousal support?</h2>
  <p>Usually yes for the recipient's remarriage. Many state statutes automatically terminate spousal support on the recipient's remarriage. Cohabitation may also terminate or reduce support in some states.</p>

  <h2>What if my ex hides income?</h2>
  <p>Discovery during divorce (subpoenas, forensic accounting) addresses this. Most states have processes for income imputation if a spouse voluntarily reduces income to avoid support &mdash; for example, by working fewer hours than their qualifications and job history would otherwise support. A forensic accountant or vocational evaluator is often brought in specifically to establish what a spouse's true earning capacity is when self-employment income or unusual perks make the number on a pay stub unreliable.</p>

  <h2>Do prenups override the formula?</h2>
  <p>Usually yes, if properly executed. Most states enforce prenuptial agreements that waive or limit spousal support, with exceptions for unconscionability or lack of disclosure. Courts typically look at whether both spouses had independent counsel, whether financial disclosures were complete at signing, and whether enforcing the waiver years later would leave one spouse on public assistance &mdash; any of which can lead a judge to set the waiver aside.</p>

  <h2>What about child support?</h2>
  <p>Spousal support and child support are calculated separately but interact. Child support usually takes priority. Some state formulas calculate them together to avoid double-counting income.</p>

  <h2>Can the judge ignore the formula?</h2>
  <p>In most states, yes &mdash; especially for permanent support. Formula guidelines are starting points, not binding rules. Judges can deviate with documented reasoning.</p>

  <h2>What counts as "income" for spousal support purposes?</h2>
  <p>More than just base salary. Most states define income broadly enough to include bonuses, commissions, overtime, self-employment profit, rental income, and investment returns. Some states also count the value of employer-provided perks (a company car, housing allowance) as income if they reduce the recipient's personal living expenses. Where it gets contested is self-employment and business ownership, since a spouse can control how much profit a business distributes versus retains &mdash; which is why forensic accounting shows up so often in higher-income divorces.</p>

  <h2>Is temporary support calculated the same way as long-term support?</h2>
  <p>No, and this is one of the most common points of confusion. Temporary (pendente lite) support, ordered while the divorce is pending, is usually set with a simple guideline formula because the court needs a fast, provisional number. Long-term or "permanent" support, decided at the end of the case, is far more likely to be a multi-factor discretionary analysis, since the judge now has full financial disclosure and can weigh standard of living, earning capacity, and the other factors a quick formula ignores. A formula-based estimate is generally more reliable for the temporary phase than for the final order.</p>

  <h2>Do all states use a formula, or is it up to the judge?</h2>
  <p>Both approaches exist, and which one applies depends heavily on the state and the phase of the case. A handful of states (for example Pennsylvania and Illinois) apply a statutory formula fairly consistently. Others, like Texas and much of New England, rely primarily on a multi-factor discretionary framework with no binding numeric guideline at all. Many states sit in between: a formula-based number for temporary support, discretion for the final order. That split is exactly why we ask which state you're in before showing a number.</p>

  <h2>How accurate is an online calculator compared to what a judge will actually order?</h2>
  <p>Treat it as a starting range, not a prediction. A calculator can apply a public formula correctly, but it can't see your judge's tendencies, your county's local practice, the credibility of either spouse's testimony, or case-specific facts like a documented history of one spouse supporting the other's education or career. Two people with identical incomes and marriage lengths can walk away with meaningfully different orders depending on those factors. Use the estimate to understand the likely range and the variables that move it, then bring your specific facts to a family-law attorney.</p>
</BaseLayout>
